> On Tuesday, June 9, 2020 at 10:50:21 AM UTC-4, pdevalpine wrote:
> > I think I follow what you are asking.  No, there isn't a way to do that.  You could do it roughly as follows:
> >
> > # in setup code, create a modelValues like mvSaved, or use mvSaved if it makes sense to.  I will call it mvBackup
> > copy(from = model, to = mvBackup, nodes = node, logProb = TRUE, row = 1)
> > oldValue <- model[[setNode]] ## or oldValue <- values(model, setNode) ## if setNode is part of node, you wouldn't need this or corresponding line below
> > model[[setNode]] <<- newValue ## or values(model, setNode) <<- newValue
> > logProb <- model$calculate(node) ## same as calculate(model, node), but preferred for style
> > model[[setNode]] <<- oldValue
> > copy(from = mvBackup, to = model, nodes = node, logProb = TRUE, row = 1)
> >
> > On Tue, Jun 9, 2020 at 7:38 AM Dirk Douwes-Schultz <dirkdouw...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > Thanks for your help Perry!
> > >
> > > I decided to leave the copy issue alone for now. I would guess copying isn't very expensive.
> > >
> > > I am more concerned about the calculate function and having to do redundant calculations. For my sampler I need to run the calculate function many times and each time it changes the internals of the model but I do not want it too. Looking at your categorical sampler  https://github.com/nimble-dev/nimble/blob/devel/packages/nimble/R/MCMC_samplers.R I see you have potentially the same issue, where you may have to run calculate one more time then necessary.  For me the problem is much worse since I have a multivariate sampler so I have to redundantly run calculate for each node to reset the model back since my calculation steps happen separately to the sampling steps.  To get around this I am currently hard coding all calculations.
> > >
> > > Is there any way to run calculate without modifying the model? Something like calculate(setNode,newValue,node) where setNode is the node you want to set to newValue for just  the calculation and node is some dependency of setNode you want calculated but without changing the model?
> > >
> > > On Monday, June 1, 2020 at 11:12:57 AM UTC-4, pdevalpine wrote:
> > > > Hi Dirk,
> > > >
> > > > Unfortunately, copy does not have the same flexibility as model$calculate to indexed subsets of node vectors.  This arises because, during the compilation process, access to the nodes is partially evaluated (preprocessed and then locked in).  Two options come to mind. The nodeFunctionList approach seems safest.  You could make a list of nimbleFunction objects with a method to do the copy for one of the dependency sets.  An alternative, if you're sure of what is going on, would be to wait until you've updated all of the cases (zt, ct?) and then do the copy for all of them together.  This would require that they don't overlap in dependencies and that they are all accepted.  If you want to send the full nimbleFunction, we may be able to suggest other strategies.  For example, if there isn't much shared computation, you could redesign this to work as multiple samplers rather than one sampler that updates multiple nodes (if I follow).
> > > >
> > > > -Perry
